# Classes vs Objects

Classes and objects are fundamental to Object-Oriented Programming.

---

# Recipe Analogy

## Class = Recipe

Contains:

- Ingredients
- Instructions

A class defines:

- Variables
- Methods
- Constructors

---

## Object = Finished Dish

An object is:

- A real instance
- Created from a class

Multiple objects can be created from a single class.

---

# Example Class

```java
public class Car {

    String make;
    String model;

}
```

---

# Creating Objects

```java
Car car1 = new Car();
Car car2 = new Car();
```

Each object has its own values.

```java
car1.make = "Toyota";
car2.make = "Ford";
```

---

# Why Classes Matter

Classes provide:

- Reusability
- Modularity
- Organization
- Maintainability

Large applications may contain hundreds or thousands of classes.

---

# Packages

Packages organize related classes.

Without packages, large Java applications would be difficult to manage.

---

# Benefits of Packages

- Better organization
- Reduced complexity
- Code reuse
- Improved maintainability
- Namespace management

---

# Package Structure Example

```text
com.company.hr

├── Employee.java
├── Manager.java
└── Department.java
```

---

# Import Statements

To use classes outside the current package:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
```

After importing, classes become available within the program.

---

# Cookbook Analogy

Think of a package as a cookbook.

Without page numbers, finding a recipe would be difficult.

Packages provide the location for classes.

Example:

```java
import com.company.hr.Employee;
```

This tells Java where to find the Employee class.

---

# Modularity in Java

Packages help create modular applications.

Benefits:

- Easier debugging
- Easier testing
- Easier collaboration
- Better scalability

This becomes extremely important in enterprise applications.

---

# Key Takeaways

- Java is both compiled and platform-independent.
- Java source code is converted into bytecode.
- The JVM executes bytecode on different operating systems.
- Java uses strict static typing.
- Primitive data types store simple values.
- Casting converts data between types.
- Classes act as blueprints.
- Objects are instances of classes.
- Packages organize related classes and support modular design.
- Import statements allow classes from other packages to be used within a program.

